Send your manuscript out for someone else to mark up in Word or LibreOffice, and bring their
comments and edits back into the project instead of a disconnected second copy. This page
covers the whole trip: what to send, how to send it, what your editor does, and how to read
the result when it comes back.

## What to send

Export to Word (`.docx`) or OpenDocument Text (`.odt`). These are the only two formats that
carry your comments out, and the only two that carry the invisible identifiers Skribisto uses
to recognize its own rows and comments coming back. A Markdown or plain text export
carries neither: an editor can still mark it up, but none of it comes back recognizable, and
it carries no comments at all.

## How to send it

Every export style (Settings, under *Compile & Export*, then *Export Formats*) has a *Sending
to an editor* group with two switches: *Include comments* and *Include round-trip markers*.
Both default to on, in the built-in styles too, so a first DOCX or ODT export is already ready
to return. Markers are invisible bookmarks, not ordinary custom attributes: a real file
round-tripped through LibreOffice had its custom attributes stripped while its bookmarks
survived. Built-in styles are read-only, so duplicate one first if you want a copy with either
switch off, say for a file that will never come back.

## What your editor does

Nothing special. They open the file in Word or LibreOffice, add comments and edit the prose as
usual, and send it back. Nothing an ordinary edit does to the text disturbs the identifiers
riding along with it.

## Bringing it back

*Work ▸ Import from ▸ Documents (Markdown, Word, ODT)…* opens one wizard: *Files*, *Review*,
*Destination*, *Merge*. See [Import documents](:help-import-documents) for the first three
steps. The round trip itself happens on the last.

## Reading the Merge step

Skribisto pairs each row in the file against the rows inside whichever part of the binder you
chose as Destination: first by its round-trip mark, if it has one; failing that, by matching
type and a normalized title. There is deliberately no pairing by prose similarity, so an
unmatched row shows as *New* rather than being guessed onto the wrong chapter.

With a mark to compare against, a paired row's Status says who changed what: *Same*, *They
edited it*, *You edited it*, or *Both edited it*. Paired by title alone, it can only say
whether the two texts agree (*Same*) or not (*Differs*), never who is responsible. A row the
file relocated shows as *Moved* instead. The Action column offers *Comments only* (their
remarks, your wording), *Take this version*, *Keep mine*, *Add as new*, or *Skip*, preselected
to whichever is safest: taking the import when they edited it, keeping yours otherwise.
*Compare* shows both versions side by side before you decide. A row your project has that the
file no longer mentions shows as *Not in this file*, with only *Keep mine* offered: a missing
chapter is not Skribisto's cue to delete it. Nothing to line up (an empty destination, say)
collapses this step to one sentence instead of a table.

## Tracked changes

A Word or OpenDocument file that comes back mid-revision arrives with its changes already
accepted: insertions kept, deletions dropped. That is the version your editor left, and the
Review step says so, naming who made them when the file records it.

Accepting or rejecting them is the Merge step's job, not a separate one. *Compare* puts your
wording beside theirs paragraph by paragraph, and a paragraph you do not tick keeps yours
exactly as it was. That is a coarser grain than Word's per-change buttons, and deliberately:
the question a writer answers is "whose sentence is this", which is a paragraph-sized
question. There is no way to take one editor's changes and refuse another's within the same
paragraph.

## Limits worth knowing

Recognition depends on marks Skribisto itself wrote. A file from another tool, or one exported
with markers off, falls back to title matching, which will not recognize a renamed chapter.
Reimporting the same file twice creates no duplicate rows or comments: the safe default is
preselected either way.

See [Export](:help-export) for choosing a style and a format, and [Comments](:help-comments)
for the notes that make this whole trip worth taking.
